.Dd November 14, 2026
.Dt INTELGPIO 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm intelgpio
.Nd Intel PCH GPIO pin controller
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device gpio"
.Cd "device intelgpio"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
intelgpio_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports the GPIO pin controller of recent Intel platform controller
hubs.
The controller is enumerated through ACPI and attaches as
.Pa gpioN .
.Pp
The pads of the controller are organized in communities, each of which holds
a number of pad groups such as GPP_B or GPP_D.
Pins are numbered the way the ACPI tables of the platform refer to them,
which leaves gaps between the pad groups, and are named after their pad,
e.g.\&
.Dq GPP_B3 .
Pad groups which firmware does not make available as GPIO pins are not
exposed.
.Pp
Pins can be read, written and switched between input and output with
.Xr gpioctl 8
and the
.Xr gpio 3
interface.
Changing the direction of a pin also takes the pad out of its native
function and puts it into GPIO mode.
.Ss Pin interrupts
A device whose ACPI description wires its interrupt to a pin of the
controller with a
.Fn GpioInt
resource gets that interrupt from
.Nm .
At present this is limited to the children of
.Xr iicbus 4 ,
such as the HID-over-I2C touchpads and sensor hubs driven by
.Xr iichid 4 ,
which fall back to polling the device without it.
.Pp
All pin interrupts are delivered through the single interrupt of the
controller, level and edge triggered pins of either polarity are supported.
A pin can only be used as an interrupt if firmware left its pad in GPIO mode
as an input and assigned it to the operating system.
Firmware may route such a pad to an IOAPIC pin too, where it does nothing
but fire the handlers of the devices sharing that IOAPIC pin.
The driver turns this routing off for as long as the pin interrupt is in use.
.Pp
The device asks for its interrupt when it attaches, so
.Nm
has to be attached by then.
This is the case if the driver is part of the kernel or loaded from
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
but not if it is loaded with
.Xr kldload 8
after the system has booted.
.Sh HARDWARE
The
.Nm
driver supports the GPIO pin controller of the following platforms:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Intel Tiger Lake-LP (ACPI ID INT34C5 and INTC1055)
.It
Intel Alder Lake-P, which uses the pad layout and the ACPI ID INTC1055 of
Tiger Lake-LP
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr gpio 3 ,
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr gpio 4 ,
.Xr iicbus 4 ,
.Xr iichid 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr gpioctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The generic Intel GPIO pin controller framework was written by
Beckhoff Automation GmbH & Co.\& KG for
.Fx .
Support for pin interrupts and for the Tiger Lake-LP pad layout first
appeared in
.Mx 4.2 .
.Sh AUTHORS
Pin interrupt support and this manual page were written by
.An Lucas Holt .
.Sh BUGS
A pin which is configured to trigger on both edges can not be used as an
interrupt.
.Pp
The virtual GPIO pad group is not exposed.
